  "version": "0.1.0",
  "note": "Curated bilingual (en+ar) behavior dictionary for the journal (EPIC-29, plan §9.9 / §11). These are the tags a user taps on the daily journal screen; anything not listed is still loggable as a free-text customLabel (same dictionary-vs-custom split as symptoms.json / beverages.json). Selection rule: every entry is (a) plausibly linked to recovery or sleep and (b) loggable in seconds. NON-JUDGEMENTAL BY CONSTRUCTION — no entry is diagnostic, moralizing, or framed as a failure ('late meal', never 'junk food'; 'less water than usual', never 'dehydrated'); the journal records what happened, it never grades it. valueKind is the shape of the answer: boolean = did it happen at all (no value, no unit), count = how many times/servings (an integer, no physical unit — it counts the behavior itself), quantity = a measured amount in `unit` (a canonical unit that exists in Units.table on both platforms). sensitiveClass is stated per behavior rather than assumed for the family so no future entry can be quietly demoted: every one of these is sensitivePlus, because a caffeine/alcohol/screen/travel pattern is lifestyle-revealing in a way a step count is not (I7) — journal rows live in badan_sensitive.db, export only under includeSensitive, and are removed by the sensitive purge. Behaviors are DISCRETE MOMENTS; a circumstance that lasts (travel, illness, fasting) is a ContextStatus interval instead (§9.10) — travel_day and felt_unwell exist here for the one-tap 'today was one of those days' case and are not a substitute for the interval.",
